{"name":"Blue Feather","hex":"#aed9ec","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"feather","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A branching, hair-like structure that grows on the bodies of birds, used for flight, swimming, protection and display.","Long hair on the lower legs of a dog or horse, especially a draft horse, notably the Clydesdale breed. Narrowly only the rear hair."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or furnish with feathers;  to fletch.","To adorn, as if with feathers; to fringe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/feather"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Feather (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Feather was a Dutch male vocal/instrumental group active during the 1980s. In 1981, they released the single \"Let's Funk Tonight\" on the Mercury label. It peaked at number 50 on the UK Singles Chart in July 1982, spending four weeks on the charts. The track was later included on the 2008 compilation album Return to the Playboy Mansion by Dimitri from Paris."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Feather_(band)"},{"title":"Billy Murray (singer)","blocks":[{"text":"William Thomas Murray was an American singer and voice actor. He was one of the most popular singers in the United States in the early 20th century. While he received star billing in Vaudeville, he was best known for his prolific work in the recording studio, making records for almost every record label of the era. Murray was the best-selling recording artist of the first quarter of the 20th century, selling over 300 million records during the phonograph era."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/BillyMurrayEdisonMay1911_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billy_Murray_(singer)"},{"title":"Feather","blocks":[{"text":"Feathers are epidermal growths that form a distinctive outer covering, or plumage, on both avian (bird) and some non-avian dinosaurs and other archosaurs. They are the most complex integumentary structures found in vertebrates and an example of a complex evolutionary novelty. They are among the characteristics that distinguish the extant birds from other living groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg/330px-Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feather"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/BillyMurrayEmerson1919.JPG/500px-BillyMurrayEmerson1919.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/BillyMurrayEmerson1919.JPG/500px-BillyMurrayEmerson1919.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Murray in a newspaper ad from 1919","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABillyMurrayEmerson1919.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Okeh4042.jpg/250px-Okeh4042.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Okeh4042.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Okeh4042.jpg/250px-Okeh4042.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"You'd Be Surprised\", on OkeH, 1919","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOkeh4042.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/LyricLabel.jpg/250px-LyricLabel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/LyricLabel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/LyricLabel.jpg/250px-LyricLabel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Oh! By Jingo\", on Lyric, 1919","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALyricLabel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg/500px-Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg/500px-Parts_of_feather_modified.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campai